His choice is not simply a rejection of his community, especially since he intends to remain and continue belonging. Still, his family’s reaction has some moral significance because shared ways of life can carry real meaning, memory, and obligation. The strongest view is that he can honor where he comes from while also becoming someone his community did not expect him to be.

For the reasoning, identity is partly inherited through people, place, and tradition, but it is also partly self-authored. The most important consideration is that belonging need not mean being completely defined by one’s community.

